@startuml proto-fm-biphase-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-fm-biphase-frame.puml - (b) the FM0/FM1 symbol rules, the one bit that
' differs, and the sixteen bytes of receiver state.
'
' There is no packet, no header, no checksum and no acknowledge in bi-phase
' coding. What there IS is a symbol rule, and the whole of the protocol is in
' it. So the breakout worth drawing is the symbol table, the FM0/FM1
' difference, and the map - because on a sixteen-byte machine the receiver's
' state IS the design.
' ===========================================================================

title FM0/FM1 bi-phase - the symbol rules, and the receiver's sixteen bytes\n<font size=12>there is no packet: a bit is two half-intervals and the value is a transition pattern</font>

hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<fm0>> {
  BackgroundColor #DCE6F5
  BorderColor #5577AA
}
skinparam rectangle<<fm1>> {
  BackgroundColor #D6F5D6
  BorderColor #3A7A3A
}
skinparam rectangle<<both>> {
  BackgroundColor #F4F7FB
}
skinparam rectangle<<red>> {
  BackgroundColor #FADADA
  BorderColor #AA3030
}

rectangle "THE RULES\nfrom which EVERYTHING here and in\ntb_pe_soc_bmc.v is written\nindependently" as R <<both>>

rectangle "<b>0</b>\n\nfirst half: | transition\nsecond half: transition\n\n<b>the SAME in FM0 and FM1</b>" as Z0 <<both>>
rectangle "<b>1</b> in <b>FM0</b>\n\nfirst half: | transition   <-- FM0's\nsecond half: NO transition\n\n<b>FM0 ADDS A TRANSITION AT THE START\nOF THE INTERVAL FOR A 1</b>" as M0 <<fm0>>
rectangle "<b>1</b> in <b>FM1</b>\n\nfirst half: NO transition\nsecond half: NO transition\n\n<b>NO transition anywhere in the bit</b>" as M1 <<fm1>>

rectangle "DECODE\nread dmem[3]" as DEC <<fm0>>
rectangle "the ONLY difference\nbetween the two encodings" as DIFF <<red>>

R -[#3A7]-> Z0
R -[#3A7]-> M0
R -[#3A7]-> M1
DEC -[#3A7]-> Z0
DEC -[#3A7]-> M0
DEC -[#3A7]-> M1
M0 -[#C09000]-> DIFF
M1 -[#C09000]-> DIFF

note top of R
  <b>FOUR RULES, AND THEY ARE THE WHOLE PROTOCOL.</b>
  ...
  1. a bit is <b>TWO half-intervals</b>
  2. a <b>1</b> has <b>NO transition at the END</b> of its interval, in BOTH encodings
  3. a <b>0</b> <b>HAS</b> one, in BOTH encodings
  4. <b>FM0 adds a transition at the START of the interval for a 1; FM1 does not</b>
  ...
  <b>RULE 4 IS THE ONLY DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE TWO ENCODINGS,</b> and therefore the
  only thing a receiver has to establish in order to know which one it is reading.
  ...
  <b>A BIT PERIOD IS A WHOLE NUMBER OF MICROSECONDS</b> - 2 us a half-interval, so
  4 us a bit - and that is not tidiness. The receiver timestamps the level on the 1 us
  counter and compares it with the level at the last CHANGE, and that comparison is
  only exact if every transition lands on a tick. A bit period that is not a multiple
  of the counter's unit would make the decoder's own arithmetic the least accurate
  thing in the act.
end note

note right of DIFF
  <b>WHY A RECEIVER CANNOT SKIP STEP 4, AND WHY THAT IS
  THE CLAIM.</b>
  ...
  A sample-based edge detector asks "did the level move?". That question is
  answered identically by all three symbols, because all three have a transition
  somewhere. <b>It cannot distinguish FM0 from FM1 at all.</b>
  ...
  What distinguishes them is a transition in the <b>middle</b> of the first
  half-interval - a data transition INSIDE a bit, not at its edge. So the decoder
  has to accumulate half-intervals and decide ONCE PER BIT rather than once per
  transition, and that is why dmem[8] counts half-intervals and the bit is
  assembled in dmem[12,13].
  ...
  <b>THIS IS WHAT THE ACT CLAIMS:</b> not "the firmware drives a bi-phase stream"
  but "the firmware RECOVERS THE CLOCK from a stream it shares no clock with, and
  SAYS WHICH ENCODING IT LOCKED ONTO".
end note

note bottom of M1
  <b>A RUN OF ONES IN FM1 IS A CLEAN SQUARE WAVE, NOT A
  DEAD LINE.</b>
  ...
  One transition per bit, in the middle, and the decoder recovers its clock from
  exactly those transitions.
  ...
  <b>THE FIRST VERSION OF tb_pe_soc_bmc.v CLAIMED OTHERWISE:</b> that a run of ones
  in FM1 was the "no transitions" stream, and therefore not a decodable frame. That
  claim was in the TB header, in the encoder below, and in two WORKLOG lines, and
  <b>it was wrong in all four.</b>
  ...
  It was the INSTRUMENT that was wrong, which is the pattern this whole block
  exists to demonstrate, and it is the reason the act was written with TWO decoders
  from the wire rules rather than one decoder and one encoder that agree by
  construction: <b>a pair that agrees by construction agrees even when both are
  wrong.</b>
  ...
  The stream that genuinely carries no clock is a line held CONSTANT - which is
  what a disconnected, shorted or dead sensor looks like - and there the firmware's
  answer must be a <b>DECLARED FAILURE (0xFF) rather than a byte it invented.</b>
  A decoder that cannot fail is not a decoder.
end note

rectangle "THE SIXTEEN BYTES, AND THE MAP IS THE DESIGN" as MAP <<both>>
rectangle "0,1,2\n<b>the three bytes\nrecovered from\nthe input pad</b>" as B0 <<both>>
rectangle "3\n<b>the ENCODING FLAG</b>\n0 = FM0\n1 = FM1\n0xFF = no transitions\n\n<b>the observable</b>" as B3 <<fm1>>
rectangle "4\n<b>the level at the last\nCHANGE</b>\n\nthe store-forward\nthe ISA cannot\nexpress - see note" as B4 <<fm0>>
rectangle "5\n<b>the microsecond\ntimestamp of the\nlast change</b>" as B5 <<fm0>>
rectangle "6\n<b>the byte this program\nENCODES onto the\noutput pad</b>" as B6 <<fm1>>
rectangle "7  bit counter\n8  half-intervals in\n     the current bit\n9  byte index" as B789 <<both>>
rectangle "10  previous 1 us\n     reading, for the\n     elapsed difference\n11  phase: 0 = decode,\n     1 = encode" as B1011 <<both>>
rectangle "12,13\n<b>the bit under\nconstruction, then the\nbyte being shifted out</b>" as B1213 <<fm0>>
rectangle "14,15  scratch for the\n     elapsed time and\n     the shift" as B1415 <<both>>

MAP -[#3A7]-> B0
MAP -[#3A7]-> B3
MAP -[#3A7]-> B4
MAP -[#3A7]-> B5
MAP -[#3A7]-> B6
MAP -[#3A7]-> B789
MAP -[#3A7]-> B1011
MAP -[#3A7]-> B1213
MAP -[#3A7]-> B1415

note bottom of B4
  <b>THE BOUNCE, AND IT IS FORCED BY THE ISA, NOT CHOSEN.</b>
  ...
  A transition detector wants a store-forward: <b>X = ram[X]</b>. On this machine it
  cannot be written. <b>OP_LDS (0xB) is <font name="Courier">a <= ram[x]</font> with NO X
  destination</b>, so the idiom is inexpressible; and the encoding that looks like it,
  <b>LDM X,addr with arg[7]=1</b>, is a <b>DIRECT</b> load whose address field is the
  <b>top nibble only</b> - it cannot even name byte 8.
  ...
  So the old level lives in a MEMORY byte, and every access to it costs a load and a
  store. <b>And that byte belongs to this decoder and to nothing else:</b> a scratch
  shared between a routine and its caller is how the DS18B20 act lost a byte counter
  to the delay routine.
end note

note bottom of B3
  <b>THREE BYTES PER DIRECTION, AND THE LIMIT IS THE
  MACHINE.</b>
  ...
  A receiver's state - the transition timestamp, the previous level, the bit
  counter, the phase flag and the frame being assembled - is most of sixteen
  bytes, and the bounce byte is most of the rest. Three bytes is what is left.
  ...
  The limit is stated here rather than discovered later, because a receiver that
  runs out of memory mid-frame produces a plausible wrong answer and nothing else.
end note

@enduml
